That source is Locke. Sandel closes by sketching the chain Nozick inherits: $\begin{aligned}
\text{self-possession}
&\to \text{ownership of labor} \\
&\to \text{mixing labor with unowned things} \\
&\to \text{private property}.
\end{aligned}$ The point of the chapter’s ending is not to settle the matter but to show exactly why the next lecture has to be about Locke. If self-ownership is the premise, then the next task is to test that premise where it first receives its classical philosophical form.

